   note:`<h3>Destination documented, route debated</h3><p>Glass’s arrival at Fort Kiowa is part of the established account. The order exercise below follows the broad traditional narrative, not a verified map of every day’s travel.</p><p>The page does not repeat the film’s invented son or make revenge the sole explanation for his survival.</p>`},
  {title:'Fort Kiowa',skill:'Speaking & reflection',deck:'Arrival is not the end of the question. It is where interpretation begins.',
   story:`<p>At Fort Kiowa, Glass returned to the human world: shelter, supplies, news, and other people. The lone survivor entered a place that existed because people worked, traded, and organized together. His story would soon travel farther than he did, attracting a devoted following in print and, eventually, on screen.</p><p>Glass went back to trapping and trading. He was killed in 1833 during an encounter with a band of Arikara on the Yellowstone River. The sober ending resists a clean movie arc. Survival did not make him immortal, and it did not settle the conflicts of the frontier.</p>`,
   note:`<h3>Historical epilogue</h3><p>Glass returned to the fur trade after 1823. The South Dakota historical account records his death in winter 1833. The arrival scene itself is reconstructed; no detailed firsthand itinerary survives.</p><p>The New Yorker discussion supplies the final reflection: survival stories can exaggerate rugged individualism even though most human lives depend on other people.</p>`}
];
const FLASH=[
  {term:'bring sth to the table',definition:'Offer a useful skill, quality, or resource to a group or situation.',example:'What skills do you bring to the table?'},
  {term:'fend for yourself',definition:'Take care of yourself without help from other people.',example:'No one should be left to fend for themselves.'},
  {term:'in a pinch',definition:'In a difficult situation, when a quick or temporary solution is needed.',example:'A little practical knowledge can help in a pinch.'},
  {term:'join forces',definition:'Work together toward a shared goal.',example:'They joined forces to find food.'},
  {term:'tap out',definition:'Withdraw from an activity because you cannot continue.',example:'He was forced to tap out of the competition.'},
  {term:'stick with sth',definition:'Continue doing or using something instead of abandoning it.',example:'She stuck with the task when it became difficult.'}
];
const CONTEXT_Q=[
  {text:'A film asks us to believe that an untrained teenager builds a weatherproof cabin in one afternoon. What does the plot do?',options:['It stretches the imagination.','It tapers off.','It comes into play.'],answer:0,explain:'Something that is difficult to believe stretches the imagination. The phrase often carries a skeptical tone.'},
  {text:'A trapper answers questions with a nod and rarely tells stories around the fire. Which description fits?',options:['A devoted following','A man of few words','Close to the bone'],answer:1,explain:'A man of few words speaks very little. It describes reserve, not necessarily wisdom or kindness.'},
  {text:'A former guide notices that he reacts a little more slowly than he did twenty years ago. Which phrase fits?',options:['He has tapped out.','He has grown up on it.','He has lost a step.'],answer:2,explain:'To lose a step is to become slightly less quick, sharp, or capable, especially with age.'},
  {text:'After weeks of cold, the travelers finally reach shelter and take great pleasure in the warmth and silence. What do they do?',options:['They luxuriate in the silence.','They strip away the silence.','They dip into the silence.'],answer:0,explain:'Luxuriate in something means to enjoy it slowly and fully. The dependent preposition is in.'},
  {text:'A joke about your own fear of being useless in a crisis feels uncomfortably personal. What can you say about it?',options:['It is in a pinch.','It is close to the bone.','It is in minute detail.'],answer:1,explain:'A remark or joke that comes uncomfortably close to a personal truth is close to the bone.'},